From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kan Zhu <kanzhu@cs.washington.edu>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 18:43:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(alignment): scope the forward range to launches, and
 state device <-> rank

Three defects the first end-to-end capture against this fork exposed.

**The forward NVTX range spanned two iterations.** It closed in
`process_batch_result`, which under overlap scheduling runs after the *next*
batch has already been dispatched -- so each range swallowed its successor's
launches and every iteration was credited with two forwards' kernels. It now
closes at the single `return ret` of `run_batch`. The range's job is to bracket
this forward's launches, not its execution: kernels are attributed by the
correlation id of the runtime call that issued them, never by when they ran, so
a range that ends when the last launch is issued loses nothing. Opening moved
below the `is_prebuilt()` early return for the same reason -- a PD-disagg
placeholder runs no forward, so it has no range to open rather than one to clean
up.

This matters beyond bookkeeping. The obvious way to make the old range correct
was to capture with `--disable-overlap-schedule`, which removes the very thing
the alignment timeline exists to measure: overlap is what puts scheduler CPU
work underneath GPU work, so a capture without it can show neither a launch gap
nor a GPU bubble. With the narrowed range a capture keeps overlap on and the
gap is directly readable -- a decode iteration's host launch window is 2.4 ms
against 4.7 ms of GPU busy, the CPU running 4.4 ms ahead.

**No iteration records were emitted at all.** `ScheduleBatch.copy()` is a
whitelist of the fields `process_batch_result` reads, and `vibesim_geometry` was
not on it; under overlap scheduling that method only ever sees the copy, so the
geometry was always None by the time it was read.

**A multi-rank capture could not be attributed.** Turning per-device kernel
streams back into per-rank evidence needs someone to say which device ran which
rank. A scheduler is handed its `gpu_id` outright, so it now emits a
`VibeSimAlignmentWorker` record stating it, before anything else can fail.
`visible_devices` rides along because `gpu_id` indexes the process's visible set
and only equals the profiler's ordinal while every rank shares one -- recording
it lets a reader detect `SGLANG_ONE_VISIBLE_DEVICE_PER_PROCESS` and refuse
rather than silently fold every rank onto device 0.

Also corrects the API request timing record. `api_first_output_wait_ms` is the
*parent* of the dispatch segments in the vLLM fork's schema, not the residual
left after them, and the reader checks that nesting. `api_e2e_ms` and
`api_response_sent_ms` are dropped: this frontend has no separable phase behind
them, and reporting a number to fill a schema slot is worse than reporting none.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
